/**
 * Sum of Digits
 * Topic: Recursion (Linear — on a number)
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS:
 * - n >= 0
 * - n = 0 → return 0
 *
 * KEY INSIGHT:
 * - Recursion doesn't only work on arrays/strings.
 *   Numbers can be reduced digit by digit using % and /
 * - n % 10 → extracts last digit
 * - n / 10 → removes last digit
 * - Number of digits in n = log₁₀(n) = O(log n)
 *   So TC and SC are both O(log n), NOT O(n)!
 *
 * =====================================================================
 * APPROACH — Tail Recursive
 * =====================================================================
 * WHY: Carry running digit sum forward in accumulator.
 *      Nothing left to do after recursive call — pure tail recursion.
 * HOW: Base case n=0 → accumulator holds final answer, return it.
 *      Recursive case → peel last digit (n%10), add to accSum, reduce n by n/10
 *
 * COMMON MISTAKE 1: Return type int but accumulator is long → type mismatch.
 *      Always use long for accumulator to handle large numbers safely.
 * COMMON MISTAKE 2: Base case returning 0 instead of accSum.
 *      accSum IS the answer at base case — never discard it!
 *
 * Call as: sumDigits(123, 0)
 *
 * TC: O(log n) — one call per digit, digits = log₁₀(n)
 * SC: O(log n) — stack depth = number of digits
 */
public static long sumDigits(int n, long accSum) {
    if (n == 0) return accSum;
    return sumDigits(n / 10, accSum + n % 10);
}
